networkreporting 0.3.2
========================
* visibility is now a declared, composable rule rather than a hardcoded
  assumption. A rule is a `fit`/`predict` pair, and three are supplied:

    - `vis_from_clique()` -- the exact rule, and the default everywhere. `y.F`
      for an on-frame alter, `y.F + 1` otherwise. It reproduces the previous
      behaviour bit for bit, so no existing estimate moves.
    - `vis_from_donor()` -- borrow visibility from a donor population, matched
      on covariates. This is the approximation that any tie which is not a
      clique needs, and it is the first rule whose fit consumes the sample.
    - `vis_coalesce()` -- try rules in priority order, recording which tier
      resolved each alter.

  `apply_visibility_rule()` applies a rule and returns a `vis_provenance`
  object alongside the values: which rule resolved how many alters, and what
  share of the *deaths* and of the *exposure* were approximated. Those two are
  different numbers and both are reported, since an approximation touching
  little exposure but most deaths is not a small one.

  Three points that shaped the design and are worth knowing:

    - A visibility that is constant within a cell CANCELS out of a rate and
      reduces the estimator exactly to the aggregate one. Visibility survives
      into a rate only through the on-frame/off-frame asymmetry -- every death
      is off-frame, exposure is a mixture -- so `vis_from_donor()` estimates a
      group size and reconstructs the split, rather than estimating a
      visibility directly. `tests/testthat/test_visibility_rules.R` asserts
      this as executable documentation.
    - The donor default is the weighted HARMONIC mean, not the arithmetic one.
      The individual estimator averages `1/v`, so `(E[1/v])^-1` is the
      functional that makes the plug-in unbiased. `"arithmetic"` remains
      available, since it is what the historical `y.F.bar / (y.F.bar + 1)`
      adjustment factor used.
    - Donor coverage failure is routine, not exceptional: `match_on` describes
      the alter but donors are respondents, and a survey of women aged 15-49
      has no donor for an alter aged 60. `min_donors` and `on_missing` make
      that loud instead of letting NA propagate into rates.

* `get_boot_ests_matrix()` now takes `visibility` and `refit`. Visibility used
  to be frozen across bootstrap replicates unconditionally. For
  `vis_from_clique()` that is correct -- visibility is a function of ego's own
  reports, not of who was sampled -- but for any rule estimated from the sample
  it understates the variance. `is_estimated` now decides, and an estimated
  rule with no refit function warns rather than silently freezing. This is a
  no-op for the clique rule, which is what makes it safe to land.

* `get_ec_reports()` splits occurrences by frame status
  (`y.DandFcell` / `y.DandnotFcell`) alongside the exposure pair it already
  produced, and warns if any occurrence is attributed to an on-frame alter. For
  mortality that is impossible -- a dead alter is never on the frame -- so a
  non-zero value means a miscoded frame indicator, a data-quality check the
  package did not previously have. Verified zero across the Rwanda 2010 DHS and
  Zimbabwe 2019 MICS prep paths. Existing column names are unchanged.

* `get_group_info()` generalises `get_sibship_info()`: the `+ 1` is now the
  explicit argument `ego.in.group` rather than a constant. It encodes "ego is a
  member of the group ego reports about", which holds for households as well as
  siblings and fails for parents and neighbours. `get_sibship_info()` is kept
  as the sibling-named wrapper.

* `add_esc_ind_vis()` is now implemented in terms of `vis_from_clique()`, so
  that it and the rule layer cannot drift apart. It also now honours its
  `varname` argument, which it previously ignored -- a caller passing
  `varname = 'foo'` silently got a column called `ind_vis`.

* new vignette, *Approximating visibility*.

* `network.survival.estimator_()` is deprecated, superseded by the spine. It
  still runs and still returns what it always did. The deprecation message
  records the two things it does that the spine cannot yet reproduce:
  estimating from aggregate reports plus known populations rather than from an
  ego X alter roster, and `within.alter.weights`.

* the tie-agnostic network survival estimator spine moved here from
  `siblingsurvival`, which now depends on this package. New here:
  `occ.exp()`, `cell_config()` and its `make.age.groups()` /
  `make.even.age.groups()` / `make.time.periods()` helpers,
  `get_esc_reports()`, `get_ec_reports()`, `get_ind_est_from_ec()`,
  `get_agg_est_from_ec()`, `get_boot_ests_matrix()`, `get_visibility()`,
  `add_esc_ind_vis()`, `sib_ic_checks()`, `nmx_to_nqx()` and `q15_to_50()`.
  Nothing in this package's own behaviour changes; the spine arrives alongside
  the existing estimators rather than replacing any of them.

  The move is a pure relocation: no expected value in either package's test
  suite was edited, and the DHS and MICS validation harnesses in
  `siblingsurvival` reproduce every published figure unchanged.

  Only the sibling-specific parts stayed behind in `siblingsurvival` -- the DHS
  and MICS prep functions, maternal classification, and `sibling_estimator()`
  itself. `get_visibility()` and the sibship helpers are named for siblings but
  are really *clique*-specific, holding for any tie that partitions the
  population into disjoint groups containing ego. A later release turns that
  into a declared, composable visibility rule; see `dev/VISIBILITY-PLAN.md`.

* this package now contains compiled code for the first time (`src/`,
  `LinkingTo: Rcpp`), for the occurrence/exposure calculation
* `Roxygen: list(markdown = TRUE)`, matching the moved documentation. Existing
  help pages are unaffected apart from whitespace, two `*...*` spans that now
  render as emphasis, and one `*` list that now renders as a bulleted list
* documented the `boot.chunk.size` argument of `report.aggregator_()`,
  `kp.estimator_()` and `network.survival.estimator_()`
* removed a stale `R/RcppExports.R` that declared `resample_stratum()` against a
  `src/` this package did not have; that routine lives in `surveybootstrap`
* adopted testthat edition 3; `tests/test_all.r` is now `tests/testthat.R`
* replaced defunct dplyr `select_()` and `rename_()` calls (underscore SE
  interface) with `select(all_of())` and `rename()` throughout
  `report_aggregator.r`, `known_population.r`, and `scale_up.r`

networkreporting 0.3.1
========================
* fixed a bug that caused a lazy evaluation-related error for the `dropmiss` 
  param when using `kp.estimator_` 

networkreporting 0.2.99
=======================
* updated `network.survival` function so that it returns cell-specific degree estimates

networkreporting 0.2
====================
* updated `nsum.estimator` function so that it can use an estimated
  total degree that has come from some source other than the dataset being
  used for scale-up estimates
* in `add.kp`, changed the name of the argument `total.pop.size` to be `total.popn.size`

networkreporting 0.1.1
======================
* very slight change to maintain compatability with upcoming
  version of dplyr

networkreporting 0.1.0
======================
* moved C++ code and other bootstrap code into a separate package,
  called surveybootstrap

* some new unit tests and vignette

networkreporting 0.0.2
======================
* rescaled bootstrap code now written in C++ for increased speed

networkreporting 0.0.1
